What this established

OpenClaw was not crashing. An external actor was deliberately calling a service restart.

11. Find the external restart source

User-systemd timers were listed:

systemctl --user list-timers --all

A custom timer appeared:

openclaw-wa-healthcheck.timer

The timer and service definitions were inspected:

systemctl --user cat openclaw-wa-healthcheck.timer
systemctl --user cat openclaw-wa-healthcheck.service

The timer was configured for:

OnUnitActiveSec=50

The health-check journal showed:

channels status failed (rc=127); assuming bridge down, will restart gateway
WhatsApp bridge down; restarting openclaw-gateway

Those timestamps aligned with the supposedly mysterious Gateway SIGTERMs.

12. Identify the watchdog bug

The script was inspected:

sed -n '1,260p' ~/.local/bin/openclaw-wa-healthcheck.sh

The key stale line was:

BIN="~/.npm-global/bin/openclaw"

That user-local OpenClaw installation had been removed earlier. The active CLI was:

/usr/bin/openclaw

The old watchdog logic treated any command failure as proof the WhatsApp bridge was down:

missing old binary
   -> command returns rc=127
   -> watchdog assumes WhatsApp failed
   -> systemctl --user restart openclaw-gateway
   -> next timer tick repeats

This explained the regular restart cycle and the intermittent ECONNREFUSED probes.

13. Correct the watchdog behavior

The old script was backed up, then changed to use:

BIN="/usr/bin/openclaw"

The safer logic also introduced these principles:

if the OpenClaw CLI itself cannot run, do not restart the Gateway;
if the Gateway service is not active, let its own systemd policy handle the service;
a failed health-probe command means unknown, not automatically “WhatsApp down”;
require multiple confirmed unhealthy probes before recovery;
apply a cooldown before another full Gateway restart;
use a lock so health-check instances do not overlap.

Manual execution was tested:

~/.local/bin/openclaw-wa-healthcheck.sh

journalctl --user -t wa-healthcheck -n 20 --no-pager

The later journal showed:

WhatsApp healthy; no action

This confirmed the corrected CLI path and health evaluation were working.

14. Reduce watchdog frequency to four checks per hour

The timer was changed from 50 seconds to 15 minutes:

sed -i \
  's/OnUnitActiveSec=50/OnUnitActiveSec=15min/' \
  ~/.config/systemd/user/openclaw-wa-healthcheck.timer

systemctl --user daemon-reload
systemctl --user enable --now openclaw-wa-healthcheck.timer

Final monitoring cadence:

15 minutes
4 health checks/hour

This reduces unnecessary probing and makes transient startup/provider conditions much less likely to create a recovery loop.

